workedExamples.question_text:
Determine the shortest path from $A$ to $E$ by inspection and state its total weight.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},x=12mm,y=12mm]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0); \coordinate (B) at (0,3); \coordinate (C) at (3,3); \coordinate (D) at (3,0); \coordinate (E) at (1.5,1.5);
\draw (A)--(B) node[midway,left] {$3$};
\draw (B)--(C) node[midway,above] {$4$};
\draw (C)--(D) node[midway,right] {$3$};
\draw (A)--(D) node[midway,below] {$5$};
\draw (B)--(E) node[midway,above right] {$2$};
\draw (E)--(D) node[midway,above right] {$6$};
\foreach \v/\p in {A/below left,B/above left,C/above right,D/below right,E/right} {\fill (\v) circle (2pt); \node[\p] at (\v) {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Use Dijkstra's algorithm to find the shortest path from $B$ to $C$ and its total weight. Explain why the final label at $E$ is smaller than the direct edge weight $BE$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},x=13mm,y=13mm]
